    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "therapy doesnt really change the fact that the person who could actually live life is just... gone. it's like i'm watching a movie of someone else's life. there's no more competition, no more movement, no more purpose. it's just quiet. a long, heavy, pointless silence. sometimes i wonder if there's even a point in trying to \"rediscover\" someone who doesn't exist anymore.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That feeling of watching a life from afar, a silent observer in your own body, is a heavy burden to carry. It makes perfect sense that you question the point of trying to find someone who feels like they've already disappeared. Instead of trying to \"rediscover\" the past self, perhaps the focus could gently shift to finding small moments of meaning right here, in this present quiet. Even within stillness, there can be opportunities for subtle connection, whether through thought, sensation, or small acts of self-kindness.",
      "source": "target",
